# APK Prototype (optional, manual only)

A prototype is a small throwaway experiment that answers one bounded feasibility question
cheaply, before an expensive durable decision. It is not production implementation, not a
completion gate, and not proof of production behavior.

## When to use it

- Only on an explicit human request that authorizes a bounded experiment, for example:
  "Use apk-prototype to check whether library X can parse format Y."
- Never activate automatically. No heuristic, scanner, work loop, or review hook may invoke it,
  and no task may require a spike.
- A normal task proceeds through the ordinary work, verify, review (if required), gate, and done
  flow without any prototype.

## Inspect repository truth first

Before spending experiment effort, read what the repository already answers:

- `apk context <task-id> --budget <units>` and the task `## Context files`;
- `apk task deps <task-id>` for dependencies and downstream consumers;
- canonical docs and existing decisions for prior conclusions.

Do not re-run an experiment the repository already settles, and do not read the whole repository
or history.

## State the bounded experiment

Write down the experiment before running it:

- the question and the hypothesis it tests;
- the concrete observation that would support or refute it;
- finite time/cost/context limits and temporary-path boundaries;
- explicit stop conditions;
- the isolated scratch space or disposable fixture that will hold the experiment.

Use owned scratch space or an appropriate disposable fixture. Keep the experiment throwaway and
outside the durable source tree unless the task explicitly allows a temporary path.

## Authorized boundaries

- No root dependency, config, or product change; no live migrations or workers by default.
- Respect external effects, real data, secrets, and existing project permissions.
- Do not silently run anything destructive; a manual invocation is not blanket authority for
  external effects or destructive cleanup.
- Never promote prototype code to production. Production work requires its own ordinary task and
  verification.

## Report observations and conclusions

Return a concise report:

- tested conditions and observed results;
- hypotheses that are supported, refuted, or still undetermined;
- production-relevant conclusions, clearly separated from the raw observation;
- limitations, unknowns, and any unverified performance or generalization claims;
- exact temporary artifacts to discard or leave uncommitted.

A successful prototype is not production verification and cannot be used as gate evidence.

## Cleanup and ownership

Cleanup touches only proven experiment-owned artifacts. Never discard user, pre-existing, or
foreign files, and never widen a cleanup path beyond the experiment's own scratch space. If
ownership is unclear, leave the artifact in place and report it instead of deleting it.

## Durable changes need approval

If the conclusions imply architecture, documentation, or task changes, propose a bounded
canonical delta with paths and ask for explicit human approval before any structural write.
Silence or refusal writes nothing. No auto-task creation, auto-commit, second decision store,
new runtime/database/state platform, or mandatory spike is introduced.

## Invocation note for harnesses

This asset is plain Markdown shipped inside the installed package under
`dist/core/templates/skills/apk-prototype/SKILL.md.hbs`. A harness without native skill discovery
must read that installed asset explicitly when a human invokes a prototype. It works with local
models and compatible harnesses; APK adds no installer, model routing, or provider integration.
